<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas Cromwell</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1485–<a href="/years/1540">1540</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Thomas Cromwell, 1st Earl of Essex</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thomas Cromwell was chief minister to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> from about 1532 to 1540. He steered through Parliament the laws that broke with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and made the king head of the Church, organised the dissolution of the monasteries and reformed royal administration and finance. He also oversaw the union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. After the failure of the king's marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anne-of-cleves">Anne of Cleves</a> he fell from favour and was executed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-cromwell.jpg" alt="Thomas Cromwell" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans Holbein the Younger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cromwell,Thomas(1EEssex)01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cromwell is thought to have been born around 1485 in Putney, then a village in Surrey. His father, Walter, was an ambitious yeoman landowner who worked as a sheep farmer and fuller, and also ran a tavern and a brewery.</p><p>Around the start of the 16th century Cromwell left his family and crossed to continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, allegedly after time in prison, and later admitted to Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-cranmer">Thomas Cranmer</a> that he had been a ruffian in his youth. Accounts of his years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> are problematic. By 1520 he was established in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> mercantile and legal circles, and in 1523 he obtained a seat in the House of Commons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Cromwell secured a seat for Taunton in November 1529 and was reported to be in favour with the King. He joined the Privy Council late in 1530. By the autumn of 1531 he had taken control of the supervision of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a>'s legal and parliamentary affairs. In April 1534 Henry confirmed him as principal secretary and chief minister, a position he had held for some time in all but name. He enforced the Act of Succession, and when Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-more">Thomas More</a> and John Fisher refused the oath, both were imprisoned and within a month executed.</p><p>In July 1536 he became Lord Privy Seal and Baron Cromwell of Wimbledon. He orchestrated the Dissolution of the Monasteries. He also secured the licence for the Matthew's Bible and obliged every church to own a Bible by 1538, and the Great Bible followed in 1539. Scholars generally agree that he was a Protestant with a Lutheran mindset. His injunctions for the Ten Articles of 1536 provoked the Pilgrimage of Grace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Cromwell arranged the King's marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anne-of-cleves">Anne of Cleves</a>, which took place on 6 January 1540 and was not consummated. Henry made him Earl of Essex on 18 April 1540, but his conservative opponents, notably the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norfolk">Norfolk</a>, used the King's anger as their opportunity. Cromwell was arraigned under an act of attainder and executed for treason and heresy on Tower Hill on 28 July 1540.</p><p>Until the 1950s historians discounted his role. Geoffrey Elton then presented him as the central figure of a Tudor revolution in government, and later historians agree on his importance if not on that term. Diarmaid MacCulloch credits him with guiding the early careers of William Cecil and Nicholas Bacon.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thomas Cromwell” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Henry VIII’s break with Rome, enacted through Parliament with the Act of Supremacy of 1534 and directed by <b>Thomas Cromwell</b>, made the king head of the English Church and extended the reach of the state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>He also reported that <b>Thomas Cromwell</b> spoke highly of it and that it had influenced Henry VIII, and a copy was possessed by Charles V.</blockquote><a href="/work/the-prince">The Prince · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Putney, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Tower Hill, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.47,-0.22&amp;year=1520" aria-label="Putney on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Thomas Cromwell"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4988 5727h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4988 5727h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.47,-0.22&amp;year=1520">What was here in 1520? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
